# Example publish denylist. Copy to ~/.claude/.journal-redact and edit.
#
#   cp .journal-redact.example ~/.claude/.journal-redact
#
# Applied by `journal.py publish` AFTER the LLM genericize pass, so a rule here
# cannot be undone by the model. Private entries keep real names; only what gets
# published to a public site passes through this file.
#
# Keep your real copy OUT of version control — a committed denylist publishes the
# exact list of names it exists to hide.
#
#   literal => replacement      case-insensitive substring match
#   literal                     becomes <redacted>
#   re:pattern => replacement   Python regex, case-insensitive, \1 backrefs OK
#
# Credential shapes and email addresses are stripped whether or not this file
# exists: private keys, ghp_*, sk-*, AKIA*, and Slack xox* tokens.

# ── THREE CONVENTIONS THAT MATTER ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. ORDER. Rules run top to bottom, so a specific composite must precede the
#    bare name inside it. With `acme` first, `acme-gateway` becomes
#    `<redacted>-gateway` instead of matching its own rule.
#
# 2. COMPOSITES STAY SINGLE TOKENS. Repo, package and branch names appear inside
#    paths — `feat/acme-gateway`, `packages/acme`. A multi-word replacement there
#    yields `feat/an internal gateway`, which is broken. Map every composite to
#    one hyphenated or snake token that is valid wherever the original was.
#
# 3. BARE NAMES GET TWO RULES:
#
#      re:TOKEN(?=[a-z0-9_/-]|\.\w)   token continues into an identifier
#                                     (`AcmeClient`, `acme_config`, `com.acme.x`)
#                                     -> one lowercase word, identifier survives
#      re:TOKEN(?![a-z0-9])           token stands alone in prose
#                                     -> the readable stand-in
#
#    `\b` is no help: it treats `_` as a word character, so `\bacme\b` misses
#    `acme_config`. Use the lookaheads above. Neither rule takes a lookbehind —
#    one would skip `myAcmeClient` and leak the name outright, and without it the
#    worst case is a cosmetically odd word.
#
#    Stand-ins are article-free (`AcmeCorp`, not `the company`) so a preceding
#    article in the original text does not double up into "the a company".

# ── repos, packages and services ────────────────────────────────────────────
acme-api-gateway          => internal-api-gateway
acme_api_gateway          => internal_api_gateway
acme-group                => example-org
widgetco-connector        => a-vendor-connector

# ── internal hosts, registries and namespaces ───────────────────────────────
re:[\w.-]*\.dev\.acme[\w.]*   => internal-dev-host
registry.acme.internal        => internal-registry
acme.atlassian.net            => jira.example.com
re:\bcom\.acme\.              => com.example.
X-Acme-Tenant                 => X-Tenant

# ── ticket keys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# In-document numbering (ADR-3, FR-2.1, NFR-7) is generic — leave it alone.
re:\bPROJ-\d+\b           => <ticket>

# ── bare org and vendor names, last so every composite above wins ────────────
re:widgetco(?=[a-z0-9_/-]|\.\w)  => vendor
re:widgetco(?![a-z0-9])          => VendorCo

re:acme(?=[a-z0-9_/-]|\.\w)      => internal
re:acme(?![a-z0-9])              => AcmeCorp
